{"text": "A strong profile begins with what the student can do and what helps the student engage. The profile lists these strengths for Aaliyah: Expressive storyteller; Strong auditory memory; Enjoys drama and environmental science; and Advocates for visual access. They give the team meaningful entry points for instruction, motivation, peer connection, and self-advocacy.\n\nHelpful preferences are: High contrast and uncluttered pages; Seat chosen for lighting, not automatically front row; Digital zoom; and Verbal description of visual events. Respecting these preferences can increase participation and support student choice.\n\nCommunication access must be planned explicitly. Aaliyah’s communication profile includes the following: Uses speech and written language; Asks 'What is on the board?' when access is poor; Prefers precise directional language; and Should receive materials, not rely on a peer reader. Staff should communicate directly with Aaliyah, allow the required processing or response time, and confirm meaning without speaking for the student. If communication becomes less effective, check access, fatigue, stress, sensory load, and the communication environment.\n\nThe key principle is to presume competence while making communication reliable. Next, I’ll explain the conditions in which Aaliyah learns best and the barriers that can make learning harder.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-E-03.mp3", "words": [{"text": "A", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.225, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 1}, {"text": "strong", "start": 0.2375, "end": 0.6, "start_char": 2, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "profile", "start": 0.6125, "end": 1.225, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "begins", "start": 1.2375, "end": 1.6875, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "with", "start": 1.7, "end": 1.85, "start_char": 24, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "what", "start": 1.8625, "end": 2.0625, "start_char": 29, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.075, "end": 2.